{"id":"646e8a8c-cf7a-4672-86fd-7c567739dc04","object":"chat.completion","created":1785031895,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    tokens = case.get(\\\"tokens\\\")\\n    calls = case.get(\\\"calls\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"tokens and calls must be lists\\\")\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each call must be a dict\\\")\\n        for key in (\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"start\\\", \\\"end\\\", \\\"score\\\", \\\"replacement_tokens\\\"):\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"missing key {key} in call\\\")\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    for c in sorted_calls:\\n        s, e = c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        overlap = False\\n        for sel in selected:\\n            if max(s, sel[\\\"start\\\"]) < min(e, sel[\\\"end\\\"]):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(c)\\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: (c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected_call_ids = [c[\\\"call_id\\\"] for c in selected_sorted]\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x[\\\"start\\\"]):\\n        start, end = c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        replacement = c[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n        result_tokens[start:end] = replacement\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids,\\n        \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a payload (which has tokens array and calls array). The objective is to select calls greedily by descending score, then start, then call_id, rejecting overlaps. Then apply replacements from right to left to produce interleaved tokens. Return selected_call_ids (list of call_ids of accepted calls) and tokens (the final token stream after replacements). The output schema requires these two keys.\n\nWe must follow the exact output contract. The function should use only standard library. No external calls.\n\nWe need to parse case: it has \"tokens\" list of strings, \"calls\" list of objects with call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. The tokens are the original text token sequence. Calls are like API calls that replace a span [start, end) (assuming 0-indexed, but check fixtures: token indices from 1 to 9? Actually tokens are t0..t9, and calls have start:1, end:3 meaning tokens[1:3]? That would be t1,t2 replaced? But the calls in fixture have replacement_tokens like [\"<a>\"] for a single token. However we need to interpret indices correctly. Usually start inclusive, end exclusive. In the fixture, start=1, end=3 would cover tokens at indices 1 and 2 (t1, t2). But the token list is 0-indexed? The tokens list has 10 tokens: index 0 t0, 1 t1, ... 9 t9. So start=1, end=3 covers t1,t2. That makes sense. We'll assume 0-based indices.\n\nGoal: rank calls by (-score, start, call_id). But careful: descending score, so higher score first. Then ascending start, then ascending call_id (lexicographic? probably string comparison).\n\nGreedy: iterate over sorted calls, if the span [start, end) doesn't overlap any already selected span, select it. Overlap means any selected call's interval intersects this one. Since intervals are half-open [start, end). Overlap if max(start, selected_start) < min(end, selected_end).\n\nThen order selected calls by start (ascending) for reporting selected_call_ids (order as they appear in the final output? Actually the schema: selected_call_ids is a list of call_ids of selected calls, order by source start for reporting. So we should sort selected calls by start ascending (and if ties? Not specified, but call_id tiebreaker maybe? Use start then call_id for stability).\n\nThen apply replacements from right to left. That means iterate selected calls in descending order of start (i.e., from highest start to lowest) to avoid shifting indices. For each call, replace tokens[start:end] with its replacement_tokens list. We need to produce a new token list.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate input: case should have \"tokens\" (list) and \"calls\" (list). Each call must have call_id (string), start (int), end (int), score (float?), replacement_tokens (list). We'll assume valid per fixtures. If missing, raise ValueError.\n\n2. Sort calls for greedy selection: sort key = ( -score, start, call_id ). Score is float, so use negative for descending.\n\n3. selected = [] list of dicts (original dict or just start, end, call_id, replacement_tokens).\n\n4. For each candidate in sorted calls:\n   - check overlap with any in selected: overlap = max(start, s_start) < min(end, s_end)\n   - if no overlap, add to selected.\n\n5. Sort selected by start (ascending). Then extract call_ids in that order to selected_call_ids.\n\n6. For replacement: make a copy of tokens list (since we must not mutate input). Then iterate selected in reverse order of start (descending). For each call, replace tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens. Since we go from right to left, indices remain valid.\n\n7. Return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": resulting_tokens}.\n\nNote: The schema requires \"tokens\" to be an array of strings. It will be the final token stream.\n\nEdge cases: overlapping intervals that are same start/end? The greedy should handle.\n\nCheck fixture: There are three calls with overlapping: a: [1,3), b: [2,5), c: [7,9). Scores: a0.8, b0.9, c0.7. Sorted: b (0.9), a (0.8), c (0.7). b selected, a overlaps (since [1,3) and [2,5) overlap), so a rejected. c selected. So selected: b and c. Order by start: b start=2, c start=7 => selected_call_ids = [\"b\",\"c\"]. Then apply replacements from right to left: first apply c at [7,9) with [\"<c>\"], then apply b at [2,5) with [\"<b>\"]. Original tokens: t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. After c: tokens[7:9] = ['t7','t8'] replaced by ['<c>'] -> list becomes: t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,'<c>',t9. After b: tokens[2:5] = ['t2','t3','t4'] replaced by ['<b>'] -> t0,t1,'<b>',t5,t6,'<c>',t9? Wait careful: after first replacement the list length changed? Actually b's interval is still [2,5) in original indices. After c replacement, list length might change if replacements have different length. In this case, both replacements have 1 token, so lengths remain same. But if replacement_tokens length differs from span length, the list length changes. So we need to apply from rightmost to leftmost to keep indices correct. Since we are doing it on a mutable list, going right to left ensures earlier indices unaffected by later insertions/deletions. So we should use list slicing assignments: tokens[start:end] = replacement_tokens. This will replace the slice with the replacement list. That works if we go rightmost first. In Python, list slice assignment can change length. So final tokens should be correct.\n\nLet's simulate: start=[t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]\nApply c (start=7,end=9): tokens[7:9] = ['<c>'] => becomes [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,'<c>',t9] (length 9, since we replaced 2 tokens with 1)\nThen apply b (start=2,end=5): tokens[2:5] = ['<b>'] => now the list has length 9, indices: 0:t0,1:t1,2:t2,3:t3,4:t4,5:t5,6:t6,7:'<c>',8:t9. So tokens[2:5] is [t2,t3,t4] replaced by ['<b>'] => becomes [t0,t1,'<b>',t5,t6,'<c>',t9]? Wait after assignment, the list length becomes 7 because we replaced 3 elements with 1. Let's recalc: original list after c: length 9. Apply b: tokens[2:5] = ['<b>'] -> that removes indices 2,3,4 (t2,t3,t4) and inserts '<b>' -> resulting list: indices 0:t0,1:t1, then '<b>', then what was at index5 now becomes index3: t5, index6->4: t6, index7->5: '<c>', index8->6: t9. So final list: [t0,t1,'<b>',t5,t6,'<c>',t9]. That seems plausible.\n\nThe expected output? The fixture doesn't provide expected output, but we can reason.\n\nThus implementation is straightforward.\n\nCode:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    tokens = case.get(\"tokens\")\n    calls = case.get(\"calls\")\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list) or not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"tokens and calls must be lists\")\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        for key in (\"call_id\", \"start\", \"end\", \"score\", \"replacement_tokens\"):\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"missing key {key} in call\")\n        # could add type checks, but not necessary for fixture\n    # sort for greedy\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        s, e = c[\"start\"], c[\"end\"]\n        overlap = False\n        for sel in selected:\n            if max(s, sel[\"start\"]) < min(e, sel[\"end\"]):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(c)\n    # order selected by start for output\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: (c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected_call_ids = [c[\"call_id\"] for c in selected_sorted]\n    # apply replacements right to left\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    # sort selected by start descending\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x[\"start\"]):\n        start, end = c[\"start\"], c[\"end\"]\n        replacement = c[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[start:end] = replacement\n    return {\n        \"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids,\n        \"tokens\": result_tokens\n    }\n```\n\nBut careful: output schema requires \"selected_call_ids\" and \"tokens\" as keys. That matches.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate input. We copy tokens.\n\nNow the overall response: must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a JSON string. So we need to output something like:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\\n\"}\n\nWe need to escape newlines and quotes properly. We'll generate the code as a string and then embed.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1654,"completion_tokens":2742,"total_tokens":4396,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2287},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":118},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}